<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Chaeronea (338 BCE)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/338-bce">338 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Chaeronea was fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> in August 338 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> and an alliance led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>. The Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> and cavalry, the latter commanded by the young Alexander, destroyed the allied army and the Theban Sacred Band. The victory gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> control of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, organised soon afterwards in the League of Corinth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaeronea-338-bce.jpg" alt="Battle of Chaeronea (338 BCE)" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kirill Lokshin; derivative work by Dipa_1965 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Chaeronea,_338_BC_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Philip II ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-sacred-war">Third Sacred War</a> in 346 BCE and concluded the Peace of Philocrates with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, which made Athens a reluctant ally. The peace was never popular. The orator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a> led the war party, and in 340 BCE persuaded the assembly to ally with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, which Philip was besieging. Philip took this as a declaration of war.</p><p>In 339 BCE a Fourth Sacred War against Amphissa gave him a pretext to march south, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> allied against him. After months of stalemate Philip marched down the main road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> in August 338 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> gives the Macedonian army as roughly 30,000 infantry and 2,000 cavalry. Philip took the right wing and placed his 18-year-old son Alexander on the left, with experienced generals. The allies, mostly Athenians and Thebans, held a line about 2.5 miles long between Mount Thurion and the Kephisos River, the Athenians on the left and the Thebans on the right. Modern opinion holds the armies about equal in size.</p><p>Diodorus says the battle was hotly contested for a long time until Alexander broke the allied line and put the right wing to flight while Philip routed the left. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polyaenus">Polyaenus</a>, of varying reliability, suggests Philip drew the Athenians forward by withdrawing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says Alexander was first to break the Sacred Band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>. Diodorus reports over 1,000 Athenians dead and 2,000 captured. Excavations beneath the Lion of Chaeronea found 254 bodies, generally accepted as the Sacred Band's grave.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Athenian orator Lycurgus said that with the dead of Chaeronea the freedom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> was also buried. Philip treated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> severely, with a Macedonian garrison and a ransom for its prisoners, but treated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> leniently, probably wanting its navy against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. In 337 BCE a congress he organised formed the League of Corinth, joined by every state except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, and voted him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> for a war on Persia. Philip was assassinated in 336 BCE, and Alexander conducted the campaigns from 334 to 323 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Chaeronea (338 BC)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Athens, urged on by Demosthenes, joined Thebes against Philip II of Macedon and was defeated at the <b>Battle of Chaeronea</b> in 338 BCE; Philip treated the city leniently, and it joined his League of Corinth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of <b>Chaeronea</b> in 338 BCE Philip II of Macedon defeated Thebes and Athens, and the Sacred Band was destroyed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A Fourth Sacred War against Amphissa in 339 BCE brought Philip south again and led to the <b>Battle of Chaeronea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Victory at <b>Chaeronea</b> in 338 BCE and the League of Corinth that followed made Philip master of Greece.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Third Sacred War against Phocis (356-346 BCE) brought Philip II of Macedon onto the council, and the Fourth (339 BCE) led to his victory at <b>Chaeronea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/amphictyonic-league">Amphictyonic League</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeating Athens and Thebes at <b>Chaeronea</b> in 338 BCE, Philip II called the Greek states to Corinth in 338-337 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-corinth">League of Corinth</a></li></ul></section>
